PLACES=[
N('orange-mills-algonquin-park','Orange Mills gunpowder works ruins (Algonquin Park)','Town of Newburgh','midh','industry','ruins',41.52242,-74.05664,'area','public','walk','','1815–1901',
  'Asa Taylor built a gunpowder mill on the Orange Lake outlet brook in 1816 (the town historian\'s marker says powder was made here from 1815 to 1901), and the Laflin & Rand Powder Company ran it after 1869. It made mostly sporting powder, though the Union Army bought some during the Civil War, and the local historian Edward Ruttenber called it the most complete works in the country. When production stopped early in the 20th century, Frederic Delano, Franklin Roosevelt\'s uncle, bought the core of the works for a park; the City of Newburgh kept it until the mid-1970s, and Orange County now owns it as Algonquin Park.',
  'Roofless stone mill buildings standing among the picnic grounds and barbecue pits beside the brook and its three ponds: fourteen of the old buildings count toward the Register district, which Wikipedia calls the only 19th-century gunpowder works left in New York. Some older houses round the park were mill workers\' homes. The town marker is at Powder Mill Road and Algonquin Drive.',
  'Fourteen roofless stone buildings of a powder works, left standing as the furniture of a county picnic park.',
  'Orange County Parks. Open during daylight hours, seasonal (Orange County tourism, read November 19, 2026; the county\'s own park page answered 403). Pavilions by reservation. A mapped lot on the park\'s land is off South Plank Road on the east side; the entrance is off Powder Mill Road near NY 52. Cronomer Hill Park is across Powder Mill Road. Stone ruins: keep off the walls.',
  [W('Orange Mill Historic District'),PICK('Algonquin Park','https://pickocny.com/explore/recreation/parks/algonquin-park'),HM('Orange Mills',259531)],
  th=[41.52272,-74.05591],thn='Algonquin Park lot, South Plank Road side',brick='no'),
N('colden-mansion-ruins','Colden Mansion ruins','Coldenham, Town of Montgomery','midh','landmark','ruins',41.52668,-74.13310,'site','view','drive','','1767–1930s',
  'Cadwallader Colden Jr., son of the colonial governor Cadwallader Colden, built a two-story Georgian stone house here about 1767, on the family\'s Coldenham estate on the old Cochecton Turnpike; a date stone read "Built by J.S. and G. Many, 3 Bros., 1767". The family sold it in 1867. It stood empty for decades, went up for tax sale in 1936, and in 1939 its English wood panelling was taken out; the Metropolitan Museum of Art shows it as the Verplanck Room. The walls fell into ruin in the later 20th century.',
  'Stone walls and foundations in the woods just off NY 17K at Stone Castle Road, propped with timber trusses (the Times Hudson Valley, 2022) and hard to see when the trees are in leaf (The Clio). A sign stands about 100 feet from the ruins at the intersection. The Colden Family Cemetery is about a mile southwest (HMdb marker 136094).',
  'The shell of a colonial governor\'s son\'s mansion, whose panelling is in the Met.',
  'Town of Montgomery, which got the 8.5-acre site from the state Department of Transportation in 2005. In August 2022 the town was shown a four-phase plan for a park here, with fencing round the ruins and a parking lot; whether any of it has been built was not checked. Treat it as view-only from NY 17K and Stone Castle Road until the town opens it; no parking lot was found on the site. Busy state highway: do not stop on the shoulder.',
  [W('Colden Mansion Ruins'),L('Times Hudson Valley: Plans unveiled for Colden Mansion park (August 24, 2022)','https://timeshudsonvalley.com/stories/plans-unveiled-for-colden-mansion-park,44297'),L('The Clio: Colden Mansion Ruins','https://theclio.com/entry/75050')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('knox-headquarters-vails-gate','Knox\'s Headquarters and the Ellison mill ruins','Vails Gate','midh','landmark','standing',41.45516,-74.04959,'exact','open','walk','','1754',
  'William Bull of Hamptonburgh built this Georgian stone house in 1754 for John Ellison, whose family milled flour here and shipped it down the Hudson to New York and the West Indies. Generals used it as headquarters through much of the Revolution: Nathanael Greene, Horatio Gates in the fall of 1782 while he commanded the New Windsor Cantonment, and above all Henry Knox, chief of the Continental artillery. It is a National Historic Landmark and a state historic site.',
  'The 1754 Ellison house, furnished to show how such a family lived; NYS Parks says the remains of the Ellisons\' mill, with traces of its underground raceway, can be visited, and the Jane Colden Plant Sanctuary lies along trails in the woods. Interior original fabric not checked.',
  'A general\'s headquarters with its owner\'s flour mill in ruins beside it.',
  'NY State Parks, run by the Palisades Interstate Park Commission. 289 Forge Hill Road at Route 94. Open Memorial Day weekend to Labor Day, Wednesday to Sunday: tours on the hour 10 am to 4 pm Wednesday to Saturday, 1 to 4 pm Sunday; groups by reservation (NYS Parks, read November 19, 2026). Grounds hours outside the season not checked. Parking not checked on the aerial beyond the site.',
  [W("Knox's Headquarters State Historic Site"),L('NYS Parks: Knox\'s Headquarters','https://parks.ny.gov/visit/historic-sites/knoxs-headquarters-state-historic-site')],
  brick='no'),
N('new-windsor-cantonment','New Windsor Cantonment and the Temple','Town of New Windsor','midh','fort','traces',41.47250,-74.05955,'site','public','walk','','1782–1783',
  'The Continental Army\'s last encampment: from June 1782 to October 1783 some 7,000 soldiers (NYS Parks adds about 500 wives and children) lived here in about 600 log huts over 1,600 acres, kept within reach of British-held New York. Here Washington talked his officers out of the Newburgh Conspiracy, and the Purple Heart was first awarded here. The town owns 167 acres of the original ground across Route 300, the Last Encampment.',
  'A reconstruction: the Temple of Virtue (the camp\'s public building, rebuilt), log huts, living-history demonstrations, and stone walls that may hold stones from the soldiers\' fireplaces. The visitor center has exhibits on the people of the camp and on Knox\'s artillery park of 1780–81. Nothing of the original huts stands.',
  'Where the Revolution\'s army waited out the peace, and Washington faced down his officers.',
  'NY State Parks, run by the Palisades Interstate Park Commission, sharing 374 Temple Hill Road (Route 300) with the National Purple Heart Hall of Honor. Grounds open year-round dawn to dusk; visitor center exhibits Monday (or Tuesday) to Saturday 10 am to 5 pm and Sunday 1 to 5 pm, through the Hall of Honor\'s entrance (the NYS Parks page gives both Monday and Tuesday as the first day; read November 19, 2026).',
  [W('New Windsor Cantonment State Historic Site'),L('NYS Parks: New Windsor Cantonment','https://parks.ny.gov/historic-sites/newwindsor/getting-there.aspx'),HM('The Temple',8281)],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('edmonston-house-vails-gate','Edmonston House','Vails Gate','midh','landmark','standing',41.45355,-74.06167,'exact','view','drive','','1755',
  'James and Margaret Edmonston came from County Tipperary in 1720 and, after seven years in Plymouth, Massachusetts, bought 200 acres here; their log cabin was for a time the only house between New Windsor and what became Washingtonville. They built a two-story stone house in 1755 and soon a stone addition. Tradition makes it headquarters for Generals Gates and St. Clair and the medical staff\'s headquarters during the cantonment, but the historian Ruttenber put the medical staff at the James Clinton house instead.',
  'A two-story fieldstone house with its stone addition on Route 94. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Managed by the National Temple Hill Association. 1042 Route 94. Wikipedia says it opens as a museum 2 to 5 pm (days not given); the Town of New Windsor lists Christmas evening openings. Otherwise see it from the road; parking not checked.',
  [W('Edmonston House'),L('Town of New Windsor: Colonial Christmas at the Edmonston House','https://newwindsor-ny.gov/New-Windsor-Connect/Town-Calendar/EventID/167/CRC/7F3951483B52681C722D1DAFA75F387A/A-New-Windsor-Colonial-Christmas-at-%E2%80%9CThe-Historic-Edmonston-House%E2%80%9D')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('washingtons-headquarters-newburgh','Washington\'s Headquarters (Hasbrouck House)','Newburgh','midh','landmark','standing',41.49778,-74.01,'site','open','walk','','1750',
  'Jonathan Hasbrouck built a three-room fieldstone house here about 1750 (an earlier farmhouse of about 1725 may have stood on the lot) and enlarged it about 1770. Washington made it his headquarters from April 1, 1782 to August 19, 1783, his longest at any headquarters of the war; the Newburgh Address that ended the officers\' conspiracy was given while he was headquartered here. New York bought it in 1850 and opened it on July 4 that year, the first historic site bought by any state.',
  'The Dutch-style fieldstone house with its jambless fireplaces, furnished as in Washington\'s time; it is closed for restoration and house tours are suspended. The 1910 two-story brick museum building has two floors of galleries, and the Tower of Victory of 1887 stands on the grounds above the Hudson.',
  'The first historic house any state bought, where Washington ended the war.',
  'NY State Parks. Enter by the main gate at 84 Liberty Street. Grounds and parking free. Mid-April to October: Wednesday to Saturday 11 am to 5 pm, Sunday 1 to 5 pm; October 21 to mid-April: Friday and Saturday 11 am to 3 pm. The Hasbrouck House itself is closed for restoration (NYS Parks, read November 19, 2026).',
  [W("Washington's Headquarters State Historic Site"),L('NYS Parks: Washington\'s Headquarters','https://parks.ny.gov/historic-sites/washingtonsheadquarters')],
  brick='?'),
N('brick-house-montgomery','Nathaniel Hill Brick House','Coldenham, Town of Montgomery','midh','landmark','standing',41.52483,-74.17616,'exact','open','drive','','1768',
  'Nathaniel Hill, born in Ireland in 1705, built this brick Georgian house on NY 17K in 1768 as his retirement home; the Register summary says the bricks had to be imported from England. Seven generations of Hills lived in it until C.B. Hill Jr. gave it to Orange County in 1975, and it opened as a museum in 1978, with the Orange County Farmers\' Museum on the land round it.',
  'A brick Georgian house with much of its original design and fittings, including some Chippendale furniture (Wikipedia). Interior not checked beyond that.',
  'A Georgian farmhouse of brick said to be shipped from England.',
  'Orange County, run with Hill-Hold as the Hill-Hold and Brick House Museums. 800 NY 17K. Wikipedia gives weekends 10 am to 4:30 pm, mid-May to early October; the museums\' own page and the county\'s answered 403, so hours not checked. A town park lot is about 330 m northwest on Berea Road.',
  [W('Nathaniel Hill Brick House')],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('hill-hold-campbell-hall','Hill-Hold (Bull–Jackson House)','Campbell Hall, Hamptonburgh','midh','landmark','standing',41.46648,-74.27282,'exact','open','drive','','1769',
  'Wikipedia says the mason Thomas Bull built this stone farmhouse in 1769; the Register list summary calls it the home of one of Thomas Bull\'s sons. It stayed in the family until Orange County took it in the late 1960s; the county runs it as a farm museum. Thomas Bull is also named, on Wikipedia, as builder of the Ellison house at Vails Gate (Knox\'s Headquarters), which Wikipedia\'s other article gives to his father William.',
  'The 1769 stone house with a summer kitchen, a one-room schoolhouse, a smokehouse and farm animals on the grounds. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Orange County, at 128 NY 416, across the road from Thomas Bull Memorial Park. Run with the Brick House; hours not checked (the museums\' and county\'s pages answered 403; Wikipedia gives summer weekends for the pair).',
  [W('Bull-Jackson House'),PICK('Hill-Hold Museum','https://www.pickocny.com/explore-orange-county-ny/outdoors/p/hill-hold-museum-6448259a18b8847a9acd988c/')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('bull-stone-house-hamptonburgh','Bull Stone House and Dutch barn','Hamptonburgh','midh','landmark','standing',41.4344,-74.2614,'site','ask','drive','','1722',
  'William Bull, a stonemason from Wolverhampton who came to the Wawayanda Patent in 1715, married Sarah Wells, Goshen\'s first woman settler, in 1718; they built this ten-room stone house on a 2,600-acre grant in the 1720s (the family marker says 1722). It has never left the family: a ninth-generation descendant lives in it, and a family association formed in 1921 looks after it.',
  'The stone house and a New World Dutch barn, which the association calls the only one still standing in Orange County. Seen from Sarah Wells Trail (County Route 8); the family marker is at Pischke Road.',
  'Lived in by the same family for three centuries.',
  'Private, the William Bull and Sarah Wells Stone House Association. Tours in person or online by appointment only, through its page. Do not go up the drive otherwise.',
  [W('Bull Stone House'),L('Bull Stone House Association','https://www.bullstonehouse.org/'),HM('William Bull',43045)],
  brick='no'),
N('jacob-t-walden-stone-house','Jacob T. Walden Stone House','Walden','midh','landmark','standing',41.56517,-74.19486,'exact','view','drive','','c. 1730s–1785',
  'The oldest house in Walden: the Register summary dates it to the 1730s, the Wikipedia article to about 1785. Jacob Treadwell Walden, a New York shipping merchant, lived here from the 1820s; he dammed the Wallkill above the falls for woollen mills and the village took his name. Since 1958 it has been the home of the Historical Society of Walden and the Wallkill Valley.',
  'A stone house on North Montgomery Street (NY 52) near Wait Street, kept as the society\'s museum. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Historical Society of Walden and the Wallkill Valley, a volunteer society, at 34 North Montgomery Street. Open to the public on a limited basis (Wikipedia); the society\'s page gives no hours, so contact it through the page. Otherwise see it from the street.',
  [W('Jacob T. Walden Stone House'),L('The Walden House','https://www.thewaldenhouse.org'),HM('Walden House',29446)],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('st-andrews-episcopal-walden','St. Andrew\'s Episcopal Church','Walden','midh','worship','standing',41.55997,-74.18994,'site','view','drive','','1871',
  'Charles Babcock, a priest and once Richard Upjohn\'s partner, designed this Gothic Revival church in 1871 on the Ecclesiologists\' ideas of how an Anglican church should be laid out. A chapel that once stood apart has been joined to it. It is still a parish, paired with St. Francis of Assisi in Montgomery.',
  'Reddish-brown brick in common bond on a brownstone foundation, slate roofs, Gothic lancet windows and a bell tower with a tall spire beside the front door, at Walnut and Orchard Streets; one lancet in the chapel transept has been bricked up. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Church property; services not checked. See it from Walnut and Orchard Streets.',
  [W("St. Andrew's Episcopal Church (Walden, New York)")],
  brick='yes'),
N('montgomery-worsted-mills','Montgomery Worsted Mills (City Winery)','Montgomery','midh','industry','standing',41.53070,-74.22602,'exact','open','drive','','1892',
  'The Wallkill was dammed here by the 18th century for a grist mill. Arthur Patchett and William Crabtree built a worsted yarn mill, extended it in 1906 and the 1920s and rebuilt the dam straight across the river in 1914. The Steinberger family bought it in the Depression and turned it mostly to making hydroelectric power; the workers\' tenements were pulled down in the late 1940s. Michael Dorf agreed to buy it in 2018 and opened a City Winery in it.',
  'A long brick mill along the river, a three-story wing over the dam and flume that houses the hydroelectric turbines, a second brick building uphill and a barn downriver. Chronogram describes the restaurant inside as an industrial space full of vintage detail.',
  'A worsted mill that turned to selling power, now making wine with it.',
  'Private business: City Winery Hudson Valley, 23 Factory Street, a restaurant and music venue open to customers. Chronogram gave Thursday and Friday 4 to 10 pm, Saturday noon to 10 pm, Sunday noon to 8 pm at the 2020 opening; current hours not checked. The mill sits at the end of Factory Street; the Wallkill Valley Railroad line crosses Factory Street near it.',
  [W('Montgomery Worsted Mills'),L('Chronogram: City Winery opens in Montgomery','https://www.chronogram.com/food-drink/rebirth-of-a-landmark-city-winery-opens-in-montgomery-10846786/')],
  brick='?'),
N('montgomery-village-wards-bridge','Montgomery village: Ward\'s Bridge, Bridge Street and Union Street','Montgomery','midh','town','standing',41.52722,-74.23900,'area','view','walk','','1792',
  'Montgomery village grew up round Ward\'s Bridge, which James Ward built over the Wallkill in the mid-1700s to reach his grist mill (gone); the village went by the bridge\'s name. Two Register districts make up its centre: Bridge Street, with 28 mostly residential buildings, the oldest of 1792, and the larger Union Street–Academy Hill district beside it, with buildings from the late 18th to the early 20th century. The Patchett House, an old turnpike way station, and the 1895 brick water works stand on NY 17K east of the centre.',
  'A walk round the old houses of Bridge Street and Union Street; the Ward\'s Bridge marker is at Bridge, Elizabeth and Ward Streets.',
  '',
  'Village streets; the houses are private. A mapped lot is on Boyd Street; on-street parking not checked.',
  [W('Bridge Street Historic District (Montgomery, New York)'),W('Union Street–Academy Hill Historic District'),W('Montgomery Water Works'),HM("Ward's Bridge",23102)],
  brick='?'),
N('harrison-meeting-house-germantown','Harrison Meeting House site and Germantown Cemetery','Town of Montgomery','midh','burial','traces',41.51114,-74.25254,'site','public','drive','','early 1700s',
  'The Palatine Germans who first settled what became the Town of Montgomery built a log church here, the Hebron Lutheran Church or Harrison Meeting, early in the 18th century; it was destroyed before the Revolution (the marker) and never rebuilt. Several of the settlers are buried inside the stone wall. The town took the neglected ground over and restored it.',
  'A walled burial ground with early gravestones and a small town plaque, at the north end of NY 416 where it meets NY 211, opposite the Orange County Airport. Nothing of the church stands.',
  '',
  'Town of Montgomery. A roadside cemetery; parking not checked (no lot is mapped nearby). Busy junction.',
  [W('Harrison Meeting House Site and Cemetery'),HM('German Town',23112)],
  brick='no'),
N('goshen-1841-courthouse-church-park','1841 Courthouse and Church Park, Goshen','Goshen','midh','civic','standing',41.40276,-74.32171,'site','view','walk','','1841',
  'Thornton Niven, a Newburgh architect, designed this Greek Revival courthouse as a twin of his Newburgh one; it was approved in April 1841 and served as Orange County\'s courthouse until 1970. Legend says the skull of Claudius Smith, a Loyalist raider hanged in Goshen, was set into the masonry over the front door; a plaque on that tradition went up in 2016. The courthouse belongs to the Church Park Historic District, the core of the 19th-century village round the green.',
  'The Greek Revival courthouse on Main Street (NY 207), the Claudius Smith plaque, and the churches and buildings round Church Park; the Register marker is at Main and South Church Streets. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Orange County offices use the building. See it from Main Street and the park; on-street parking and a lot on Court Lane.',
  [W('1841 Goshen Courthouse'),HM('1841 Courthouse',42439),HM('Church Park Historic District',138560)],
  brick='some'),
N('historic-track-goshen','Historic Track and the Harness Racing Museum','Goshen','midh','amuse','standing',41.40395,-74.31896,'site','open','walk','','1838',
  'Races were run down Goshen\'s Main Street from the 1750s; the track site was first used in 1838 and the present half-mile oval dates from 1873, which makes it the oldest harness track in use in North America. President Grant came to the races in June 1873. Parimutuel betting ended in the late 1970s, but races are still held round the Fourth of July and Labor Day. The museum next door, opened in 1951, is in a half-timbered stable built by J. Howard Ford in 1913.',
  'The oval, its stables (still in use) and the 1913 stable that houses the museum, with sulkies, paintings, Currier & Ives prints and a preserved stable you can walk through.',
  'The oldest harness track in North America, still racing.',
  'The Harness Racing Museum & Hall of Fame (its own body, not public land). Museum: 240 Main Street, Tuesday to Saturday 10 am to 4 pm, $10 adults, $5 children 4 to 12; closed Christmas, New Year\'s Day, Easter and Thanksgiving; park on the street or behind the museum (harnessmuseum.com, read November 19, 2026). The track is run by its own body; race days only, not checked further.',
  [W('Historic Track'),W('Harness Racing Museum & Hall of Fame'),L('Harness Racing Museum: hours and admission','https://harnessmuseum.com/node/19'),HM('Harness Racing Museum & Hall of Fame',147597)],
  brick='no'),
N('old-stone-schoolhouse-goshen','Old Stone Schoolhouse (District School No. 9)','Town of Goshen','midh','civic','standing',41.376309,-74.349536,'exact','view','drive','','1700s–1936',
  'A one-room stone school that the Register dates to at least the 1790s and the DAR marker to the 1700s; it was used as a school until 1936, when the Minisink Chapter of the Daughters of the American Revolution bought it. Local legend says Washington stopped to talk to the pupils as he rode by.',
  'A small stone schoolhouse beside Route 17A, about 0.4 mile from Gibson Road, with the DAR marker. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Minisink Chapter, NSDAR. Opening times not checked; see it from Route 17A, where traffic is fast.',
  [HM('Old Stone Schoolhouse',29728)],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('hopewell-presbyterian-thompson-ridge','Hopewell Presbyterian Church','Thompson Ridge, Town of Crawford','midh','worship','standing',41.57071,-74.33493,'exact','view','drive','','1831',
  'The congregation was founded in 1778 in a stone church a mile and a half west; it moved into this stone Gothic Revival church in 1831. An addition was built at the back in 1968.',
  'A stone Gothic Revival church at the junction of Thompson Ridge Road (County Route 17) and NY 302. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A working church; services not checked. See it from the road.',
  [W('Hopewell Presbyterian Church (Crawford, New York)')],
  brick='no'),
N('brotherhood-winery','Brotherhood Winery cellars','Washingtonville','midh','industry','standing',41.42833,-74.16139,'site','open','drive','','1839',
  'Jean Jacques, a French immigrant, started it in 1816 and made the first commercial vintage in 1839, so it is generally called the oldest working winery in the United States (its marker gives 1839 for the founding). His sons ran it as Jaques Brothers; James and Edward Emerson bought it in 1886, renamed it Brotherhood, put up the one original building left on the property and dug the large underground cellars. It kept going through Prohibition making sacramental wine.',
  'The underground cellars, dug by hand in the late 19th century, with more than 200 oak barrels and a vaulted room holding old vintages, seen on the tour (the winery\'s page).',
  'Cellars dug by hand under America\'s oldest working winery.',
  'Private business, open to the public. April to December: Tuesday to Thursday and Sunday 11 am to 5 pm, Friday and Saturday 11 am to 6 pm; January to March: Friday to Sunday 11 am to 5 pm (Thursday shop only). Tours Tuesday to Sunday through the day, walk-in, $25 (brotherhood-winery.com, read November 19, 2026). Closed Thanksgiving and Christmas.',
  [W('Brotherhood Winery'),L('Brotherhood Winery: tours and hours','https://brotherhood-winery.com/tours'),HM("America's Oldest Winery",105637)],
  brick='?'),
N('moodna-viaduct','Moodna Viaduct','Salisbury Mills, Cornwall','midh','rail','standing',41.430519,-74.099259,'site','view','drive','','1906–1909',
  'The Erie Railroad built this steel trestle in 1906–08 for its Graham Line freight bypass and opened it in January 1909. It is 3,200 feet long and 193 feet high, the highest and longest railroad trestle east of the Mississippi still carrying trains; its open design keeps the wind off it. It also spans the Erie\'s abandoned Newburgh Branch.',
  'The trestle striding across the Moodna Creek valley at the north end of Schunemunk Mountain; the HMdb marker is on Orrs Mills Road, which passes under it, as does Otterkill Road.',
  'The tallest railroad trestle in the East still in use.',
  'Active railroad (Metro-North Port Jervis Line and Norfolk Southern freight): view only from Orrs Mills Road or Otterkill Road, never on the tracks or trestle. Parking not checked; pull off only where the road allows.',
  [W('Moodna Viaduct'),HM('Moodna Viaduct',24272)],
  brick='no'),
]
